```moonbit
///|
trait MyTrait {
  example(Self) -> Unit
}

///|
struct MyStruct {}

///|
impl MyTrait for MyStruct with example(self) {

}

///|
fn check_if_equals(left : &MyTrait, right : &MyTrait) -> Bool {
  physical_equal(left, right)
}

///|
fn main {
  let value = MyStruct::{  }

  println(check_if_equals(value, value))
  // false

  let value_as_trait : &MyTrait = value
  println(physical_equal(value_as_trait, value_as_trait))
  // true
}
```

This is due to how the compiler currently just emits `ref.eq` instructions in-place for `physical_equals` and each time you cast to a trait reference the compiler creates a wrapper Wasm GC `(struct)` instance with effectively a vtable, so the two GC objects won't be equivalent if they weren't created from the same cast.

If you happen to use the same Wasm GC `(struct)` instance the compiler emits when you cast to `&MyTrait` then the result will be `true`, like in my second example above.

