Moonbit's current enum allowing each variant to have distinct constructors and associated data. However, this design can feel cumbersome in scenarios where we want to define a set of values with uniform fields (akin to Java's enum classes). In Rust, accessing data carried by an enum variant requires explicit pattern matching rather than direct field access.

```moonbit
enum WeekOfDay {
    Monday ( index~: Int, name~: String )
    Tuesday ( index~: Int, name~: String )
    Wednesday ( index~: Int, name~: String ) // all constructors have the same fields
    // ... other days
}

fn main {
    // create an instance of Monday, but there can only be one Monday
    let day = WeekOfDay::Monday(index=1, name="Monday"); 
    let index=match day { // to get the index of the day
        WeekOfDay::Monday ( index~, .. ) => index
        WeekOfDay::Monday ( index~, .. ) => index
        WeekOfDay::Monday ( index~, .. ) => index
	_ => -1
    };
}
```

This approach is verbose both in definition and usage, especially when all variants share the same field structure. In contrast, Java handles this more concisely:
```java
enum WeekOfDay {
    MONDAY(1, "Monday"),
    TUESDAY(2, "Tuesday"),
    WEDNESDAY(3, "Wednesday");

    final int index;
    final String name;
    WeekOfDay(int index, String name) {  // only one constructor, declare once
        this.index = index;
        this.name = name;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WeekOfDay day = WeekOfDay.MONDAY; // get an instance without creation
        System.out.println("Index: " + day.index); // direct field access
    }
}
```
I propose adding a syntax sugar or extension to Moonbit enums that supports "uniform constructor" enums. And `index` and `name` can be provided as enum values' builtin filelds.
```
enum WeekOfDay(index~: Int, name~: String) {
    Monday(1, "Monday"),
    Tuesday(2, "Tuesday"),
    Wednesday(3, "Wednesday"),
}

fn main {
    let day = WeekOfDay.Monday; // get enum instance
    println(day.index); // Direct field access
    println(day.name);
}
```

